Parallel audio processing in MusE: findings and plan
====================================================

Status: all four steps are done (measuring, the executor off by default, the fixes for the plugin hosts, settings).


HOW THE AUDIO IS PROCESSED NOW
------------------------------

* Audio::process1 (muse/audio.cpp) runs the aux tracks, then every AudioOutput::process, then the tracks
  that nothing pulled from. All in the audio thread.
* The data is pulled. AudioTrack::copyData (muse/node.cpp) calls getData, which calls copyData of every track
  in the in-routes (TRACK_ROUTE). Each track is processed once per cycle: the first call does the work and
  sets the processed flag (cleared by preProcessAlways), later calls copy the finished outBuffers.
* process1 already processes tracks that have no consumer by calling copyData with a dummy buffer. A
  scheduler can use the same call to run tracks ahead of whoever pulls from them.

What a track depends on
  * the tracks in its in-routes (also the audio inputs of a synth track, the sidechain of a VST3 instrument),
  * for an aux track: every track that has aux sends (AudioAux::getData, muse/audiotrack.cpp).
  MIDI is processed before the audio, wave tracks read their own prefetch fifo, and the metronome has no
  inputs, so they are leaves. The routes cannot make a cycle (they are checked when they are made).

Things that stop a parallel run from being correct, found by reading the code
  1. Aux send buffers: every track adds its send into the one AudioAux::sendBuffer (muse/node.cpp, in
     copyData). Needs one buffer per sending track, summed by the aux track in a fixed order, which also
     keeps the result the same from run to run.
  2. The VST2 host callback has a static VstTimeInfo (muse/vst_native.cpp) shared by all instances.
  3. Plugins: DSSI, LV2, LADSPA, DSSI-VST hosts still have to be audited, and some plugins are not safe to
     run beside each other. Needs a way to say "process this one in the main audio thread".
  Fine as they are: meters, latency compensators and plugin pipelines are per track, the audio to GUI
  rings (song->putIpcCtrlGUIMessage and the like) take several writers, MusE sets no denormal flags.


PLAN
----

Step 1: measure                                                                       DONE
  muse/audioprofile.{h,cpp}. Off by default. MUSE_AUDIO_PROFILE=<seconds> prints a report to stderr every
  that many seconds (about 20 heart beats a second, so the interval is rounded to 50 ms):
    * the number of cycles, the frames per cycle, how long a cycle may take, and how long a whole pass over
      the tracks (process1) takes,
    * for every audio track: its own time per cycle (without the tracks it pulls from), the longest single
      run, and the tracks it needs,
    * the sum of all the times, the longest chain of tracks that need each other, and the most parallel
      processing could gain (sum / longest chain),
    * an estimate of the time of a pass with 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8 and 12 threads, from a simple scheduler that
      gives the ready track with the longest way left to the end to a free thread. It leaves out the cost of
      handing work to other threads.
  Cost when off: a flag test per track and per cycle. The time of a track is taken in ProfileScope
  (muse/node.cpp, in copyData, after the processed flag is set) and the outer track's time stops while an inner
  one runs.
  Example, 9 VST3 synths (Surge XT, Diva, Six Sines) in two groups into one output, each holding a note,
  512 frames at 44100 Hz (a cycle may take 11.6 ms), run with muse4 -a -D under X11:
    a whole pass takes 3.6 ms on average (31 % of a cycle), the longest 6.5 ms
    each synth takes 0.24 to 0.61 ms, groups and output about 5 us
    longest chain 0.62 ms, so at most 5.7x; estimated 2 threads 1.89x, 3: 2.97x, 4: 3.44x, 6: 5.09x
  (That is a project made for the test, the numbers of a real one will differ. They show that the
  profile works and that many independent synth tracks can scale. Mostly serial chains do not.)

Step 2: an executor, off by default                                                    DONE
  muse/audioparallel.{h,cpp}. Turn it on with MUSE_AUDIO_THREADS=<n> (n threads including the audio thread,
  2 to 16). The worker threads are started from the heart beat once the audio device is up, with SCHED_FIFO at
  MusEGlobal::realTimePriority if that works (otherwise normal threads and a warning).
  * Audio::process1 calls audioParallelProcess before the serial passes. It processes every audio track that
    is not an output by calling copyData with a dummy buffer, as the serial "leftover" pass does, but ahead of
    whoever pulls from it and on several threads. The serial passes then find those tracks done, and only the
    outputs are left for them.
  * The graph (in-routes + aux sends, see above) lives in fixed arrays (1024 tracks, 16384 edges) and is
    rebuilt in the audio thread when a signature of the tracks and routes changes, so nothing is allocated
    there. A cycle, or more than the limits, means serial processing.
  * Order: a thread takes the ready track with the longest chain of tracks after it. All threads, the audio
    thread too, loop until all tracks are done. Workers sleep on a semaphore between cycles.
  * Safety net: AudioTrack::copyData claims the track with a compare-exchange on _processed. A thread that
    finds a track claimed by another waits (AudioTrack::waitDone, at most 50 ms, only while the executor runs)
    until _done is set, so a dependency that is missing from the graph costs time and not correctness.
  * Aux sends: the tracks no longer add into the aux track's buffer. AudioAux::getData waits for all the
    sending tracks, then adds their cached output buffers times the send level, in track list order. The
    result is the same from run to run and does not depend on which thread ran what. (This also changes
    the serial mode: the sum is in track list order, where it was in processing order before. Same values,
    maybe a different last bit.)
  Result on the nine synth test project (4 threads, 512 frames, 44100 Hz): a whole pass 4.0 to 5.1 ms
  serial, 1.6 to 1.9 ms with 4 threads, no timeouts. The aux sends reach the aux tracks in both modes (a
  project with two aux tracks and sends on all tracks was checked by looking at the aux buffer peak; the
  audio was not compared sample by sample, the synths are not repeatable run to run).
  Not done yet: falling back to serial when the profile says it does not pay (a minimum of 3 tracks is
  used), a spin before the semaphore wait to cut the wake up time.

Step 3: fix what step 2 needs                                                          DONE
  * Aux sends summed by the aux track, no shared buffer (see step 2).
  * VST2: VstTimeInfo is thread_local. The host callbacks go to the instance (effect->user), the other
    statics (currentPluginId and its semaphore) are only used while a plugin is created.
  * LV2: the plugin state is per instance; the URID map has a mutex, unmap now takes it too (it read the map
    while another thread could add to it). lilv is only called from the audio thread for connect_port/run.
  * DSSI, LADSPA, MESS synths: per instance, no shared state found (read the process paths).
  * The pool that the audio thread's MidiPlayEvent sets allocate from (libs/memory TypedMemoryPool, one
    per type and shared by all synths) was not thread safe. It crashed within seconds with LV2 synths on
    several threads. Now it has a spin lock around alloc and free (a few instructions).
  * The reference count of EvData (sysex) is atomic, events are copied between threads.
  * A track can be marked "Process in the Audio Thread Only" (track list, right click on the name, audio
    tracks; saved in the song as <processSerially>). Such a track is a node that only the audio thread takes,
    for a plugin that does not work beside others.
  Tested with the plugins on this machine, each kind in a project of 56 synths and 9 effects in group racks,
  8 and 16 threads and a few runs each, no crashes, no wait timeouts:
    LADSPA effects, DSSI effects and synths (Kars, Nekobi), native VST2 effects and synths (Kars, Nekobi),
    LV2 effects and synths (Kars, Nekobi, synthv1, drumkv1, ZynAddSubFX, reasonablesynth, mda Piano),
    MESS synths (Organ, DeicsOnze, vam), VST3 (earlier test), with notes played on all synths
    (MUSE_AUDIO_TEST_NOTES=1 plays a note on every synth track now and then).
  Not tested: DSSI-VST (wine), plugins with their own GUI threads while running, fluidsynth, a real
  time priority run (the workers fall back to normal threads without it).
  The speed up on that project is small (a pass 1.7 ms serial, 1.0 to 1.1 ms parallel), the synths there
  take 10 to 80 us each, so handing work to threads costs about as much as it saves. The plugin racks of
  the two groups are the longest chain. It pays for tracks that take longer, like the VST3 test.

Step 4: settings, measuring, display                                                   DONE
  * Settings > Global Settings > Audio: "Parallel audio threads" (Off, or 2 to 16, including the audio
    thread) and "Use the threads only when it is faster". Saved in the configuration (audioThreads,
    audioThreadsAuto). Takes effect at once: the heart beat starts missing workers and tells the audio thread
    how many to use, nothing needs a restart. Workers are never stopped, they sleep on a semaphore.
  * Measuring (audioParallelProcess / audioParallelEnd): a cycle is timed from before the tracks to the end of
    Audio::process1, in parallel and in serial mode. After 8 cycles of each, and every 32nd cycle for the mode
    not in use, the averages (exponential, 1/8) decide. Serial is the safe mode: parallel is chosen only if it
    is 10 % faster, and dropped as soon as it is slower. The numbers restart when the tracks or routes change
    or the number of threads does.
  * The settings page shows what is happening (off, measuring, parallel with the times of both, serial because
    it is not faster, not used because there are fewer than 3 tracks), the number of tracks and the longest
    chain, and a warning if the worker threads have no real time priority. Updated every second while the
    page is open.
  * Environment variables, for trying: MUSE_AUDIO_THREADS=<n> overrides the setting and turns the measuring off
    (always parallel), MUSE_AUDIO_THREADS_AUTO=0|1 overrides the measuring setting. With MUSE_AUDIO_PROFILE the
    choice is also printed every five seconds. MUSE_AUDIO_TEST_NOTES=1 plays notes on all synths.
  Tried: forcing 4 threads, the setting from the configuration file, the auto mode on the nine synth project
  (pass 2.1 ms parallel against 5.2 ms serial, stays parallel), on 56 synths (0.7 ms against 1.7 ms) and on
  three cheap synths (63 against 56 us, goes serial). The settings page was looked at in a screenshot.
  Not done: the per track times of the profile are still only on stderr (MUSE_AUDIO_PROFILE).
